and

REGENT OF LABUHANBATU SELATAN

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 40

1. The Regent coordinates the protection of Women and Children against acts of Violence with:
a. the government;
b. regency/city regional governments;
c. vertical agencies;
d. educational institutions; and
e. community institutions.
2. Coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. religious affairs;
b. health affairs;
c. education affairs;
d. social affairs;
e. legal affairs;
f. labor affairs; and
g. other affairs.
3. Coordination as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their authority.

Article 16

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Community as referred to in Article 13 letter b, are implemented in the form of Community participation.
2. Forms of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), include:
a. forming family partners at the Village/Sub-district level;
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of the rights of Women and Children;
d. providing first aid to Victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authority if Violence against Victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, community self-help institutions, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
4. Forms of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are carried out responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 32

1. Fulfillment of social welfare of Women and Children for prevention as referred to in Article 31 paragraph (1) includes:
a. enhancement of the Regional Government's role in the protection of Children including Children with special needs, Children with disabilities and other vulnerable Children;
b. raising Community awareness including Children regarding the protection and participation of Women and Children;
c. capacity building of the Community, Family, including Children and social welfare institutions, regarding Child protection;
d. capacity building of protection services for Women and Children;
e. guarantee of continuity of education in accordance with the 12 (twelve) year Compulsory Basic Education program; and
f. guarantee of obtaining basic health Services.
2. Fulfillment of social welfare of Women and Children for reducing vulnerability risks, includes:
a. identification and/or early detection of vulnerable Women and Children;
b. mediation and counseling for vulnerable Women and Children;
c. services and facilitation of support for vulnerable Women and Children; and d. care support for Children who need alternative care.
3. Fulfillment of social welfare of Women and Children for handling Victims, includes:
a. fulfillment of the rights of Women and Children who become Victims;
b. referral services;
c. rehabilitation; and d. reintegration.
4. Handling of Child Victims as referred to in paragraph (3) have the right to receive protection from local mass media coverage both electronic and print to ensure the future of the Child.

Article 4

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. respect and protect Human Rights;
b. guarantee the rights of every Woman and Child to survival, growth and development;
c. non-discrimination;
d. protect Women and Children from threats, fear, torture and or treatment that degrades the degree, self-esteem and dignity of humanity;
e. prevent forms of violence against Women and Children;
f. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against Women and Children;
g. protect and provide a sense of security for Women and Children;
h. provide services to Women and Children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses;
i. protect and prevent all forms of discrimination against Women and Children in the region;
j. Women's Empowerment;
k. the best interests of the Child; and
l. realize a region that is suitable for Women and Children.

Article 21

The Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of Violence against Women and Children, through:
a. socialization activities of laws and regulations to the Community related to the protection of the rights of Women and Children;
b. conducting empowerment and awareness to Families, parents and the Community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling. c. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social Services;
e. opening employment opportunities for Women;
f. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence;
g. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
h. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various community non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about Women and Children;
i. opening complaint posts for the protection of Women and Children from acts of Violence; and
j. training of P2TP2A members related to the implementation of their duties in preventing Violence against Women and Children.

Article 10

Neglect as referred to in Article 6 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of a Child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a Child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within the scope of their Household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the Victim is under the control of that person.

3.6.text:
Article 19

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 18 consists of elements of the Community, universities, professional organizations, community organizations concerned with the Community.
2. The establishment of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Regent's decree.
3. The implementation of Services to Victims is carried out in an integrated manner by Village/Sub-district P2TP2A, District P2TP2A, and Regency P2TP2A.
4. P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the duty to:
a. receive complaints/reports of Violence against Women and/or Children, consultation, and counseling;
b. receive and send to other Service units in a tiered manner;
c. provide legal assistance;
d. health;
e. Social Rehabilitation;
f. legal Services;
g. repatriation and social reintegration; and
h. provide protection to Victims, reporters and witnesses.
5. P2TP2A may provide temporary services and protection in the form of a Safe House.
6. In the event that P2TP2A does not yet have a Safe House as referred to in paragraph (5), then Victims of Violence are referred to P2TP2A that has a Safe House.
7. P2TP2A consists of several Divisions consisting of:
a. Complaint Service division;
b. health Service division;
c. legal aid consultation; and
d. rehabilitation, Repatriation and Social Reintegration.

Article 23

Forms of P2TP2A Services include:
a. Medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of Victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. Psychosocial services are services provided in order to restore the traumatic condition of Victims, including the provision of Safe Houses to protect Victims from various threats and intimidation for Victims and provide social support so that Victims have self-confidence, strength and independence, in resolving their problems;
c. Medico-legal services are forms of medical services for the purposes of evidence in the legal field;
d. Legal services to assist Victims in undergoing legal processes; and
e. Economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that Victims can be independent.

Article 26

1. Forms of Services provided to Women and Children Victims of Violence, are as follows:
a. Complaint services;
b. Health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medico-legal;
f. Identification services; and
g. Psychological services.
2. Forms of Services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. Women's empowerment and Child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
3. In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other district/city governments, the Community, Families and parents.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for Services, and handling of Women and Children Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraphs (1), (2), and (3), are regulated by Regent Regulation.

